@* Covers the attribute splatting of the text: the class and the style the component builds itself are merged with
   the ones splatted through a dictionary rather than replacing them, and every attribute the component would
   otherwise write as null is resolved against the splatted spelling of the same name instead of removing it.

   The dictionary is the only way to reach these: the razor compiler matches a plain "class" or "dir" written on a
   component to the parameter of that name, case insensitively, so they never arrive as splatted attributes. *@

@* A key of the dictionary matches the parameter of that name case insensitively and is written after it, so a
   "class" or a "style" splatted here replaces the Class and the Style parameters rather than joining them. What
   the merge keeps is the class list and the style the component builds of its own - the root class, the variant,
   the alignment - which would otherwise be thrown away by the splatted attribute of the same name. *@
<BitText Element="p" Align="BitTextAlign.Center"
         @attributes="@(new Dictionary<string, object>
                        {
                            ["class"] = "splatted-class",
                            ["style"] = "font-weight:bold",
                        })">
    I'm a merged text
</BitText>

<BitText Element="section"
         @attributes="@(new Dictionary<string, object>
                        {
                            ["id"] = "splatted-id",
                            ["dir"] = "rtl",
                            ["lang"] = "de",
                            ["aria-label"] = "splatted label",
                            ["tabindex"] = "-1",
                            ["aria-level"] = "2",
                            ["role"] = "note",
                        })">
    I'm a splatted text
</BitText>

@* The dictionary is written first here, so the parameters beside it are the later of the two spellings and are
   the ones kept - which is how a page gives a component a default set of attributes and overrides one of them. *@
<BitText @attributes="@(new Dictionary<string, object>
                        {
                            ["id"] = "splatted-id",
                            ["dir"] = "rtl",
                            ["lang"] = "de",
                            ["aria-label"] = "splatted label",
                            ["tabindex"] = "-1",
                            ["aria-level"] = "2",
                            ["role"] = "note",
                        })"
         Element="article"
         Id="own-id"
         Dir="BitDir.Ltr"
         Lang="fa"
         AriaLabel="own label"
         TabIndex="0"
         AriaLevel="5">
    I'm an overriding text
</BitText>
